WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.440
Hopefully you've seen that there are

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.480
many ways of working on a mainframe.

3
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.240
We're no longer in the days where

4
00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.600
everything needs to be a green screen terminal.

5
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.340
It's there if that's what you like,

6
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.240
but today we can be as graphical,

7
00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.800
text, or API based as you'd like.

8
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.840
After all, the fundamentals of

9
00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.680
ZOS are well-documented and

10
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.700
open to whatever method you want to use.

11
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.640
Let's talk about ZOSMF,

12
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.440
which stands for the ZOS Management Facility,

13
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.240
and it brings things together for some of these approaches.

14
00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.600
ZOSMF provides system management functions and

15
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.600
a task-oriented browser-based user interface.

16
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.320
The goal here is twofold.

17
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:45.680
One, making access to the system as easy as opening a browser,

18
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.320
and two, making the controls you need

19
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.800
make sense for how you'll be

20
00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.000
using them in a modern context.

21
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.760
This includes streamlining

22
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.280
the kinds of commands we've been talking about here,

23
00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.800
but there's also a lot of

24
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.160
new capabilities that are unique to ZOSMF itself.

25
00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.640
ZOSMF is shipped as part of the operating system,

26
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.000
and it aims to simplify, optimize,

27
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.920
and modernize ZOS to the system programmer,

28
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.520
as well as to people who specialize in networking,

29
00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:14.960
storage, security, the list goes on.

30
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.160
There's really something for everyone.

31
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.800
I can hear what you're saying.

32
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.160
If this is simpler and more streamlined,

33
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.040
why didn't you just show me this earlier?

34
00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.280
Why did I have to learn about IODF,

35
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.160
configs, libraries, and JCL and all that?

36
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.200
Well, in many cases,

37
00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.160
even if the functionality of something

38
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.000
is brought into a single button,

39
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.320
it's really helpful to know

40
00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.120
just what that button is doing,

41
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.680
just in case you need to debug or troubleshoot a larger issue.

42
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.080
The goal here is simplification, assistance,

43
00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:47.080
and scriptability, not just hiding the in-between steps.

44
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.200
For example, installing software

45
00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.000
using the software management task

46
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.280
guides you through each step of a checklist.

47
00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.040
And then once the installation

48
00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.040
takes place and deployment is complete,

49
00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.400
you have a greater assurance

50
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.520
that everything landed in the right place

51
00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.840
and that the installation has been verified

52
00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:05.960
by the provided ZOSMF workflows.

53
00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:09.200
Software updates can also be handled through ZOSMF.

54
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.520
Just select what needs updating through the GUI,

55
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.400
the target zone, and the paths you'd like to use,

56
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.680
and then follow the guided steps.

57
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.600
ZOSMF is useful for corrective installs,

58
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.200
installing recommended updates,

59
00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.840
as well as functional updates

60
00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.840
for enabling features or functional categories.

61
00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:28.520
We can even integrate ZOSMF with Ansible

62
00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.200
by leveraging the cloud provisioning

63
00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.520
and management for ZOS function in ZOSMF.

64
00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.640
This includes some Ansible roles you can use

65
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.160
to drive the provisioning and management of software

66
00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.160
like Kix, DB2, MQ, IMS, WebSphere Liberty, and ZOS Connect.

67
00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.600
This is just a way to make it even easier

68
00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.480
to connect a ZOS environment to an Ansible ecosystem.

69
00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:51.880
You'll really see the benefit

70
00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:53.720
when you have a mixed skill workforce

71
00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:55.920
because the experience system programmer

72
00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.760
can take a task they know, like the back of their hand,

73
00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.400
and automate it into what's called a workflow,

74
00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.520
and then hand that workflow off to a new main framer,

75
00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:06.280
and they can guide it through completion

76
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.120
without needing to come up with the procedure

77
00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.600
all by themselves.

78
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.800
Even better, because the workflow has already been written

79
00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.360
and we all know it works,

80
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.920
it's going to be a whole heck of a lot more consistent

81
00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.200
from run to run than someone typing out commands for memory.

82
00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.600
You don't need me to tell you that's important.

83
00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.760
These workflows integrate with JCL,

84
00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.000
Recs, Unix, REST APIs,

85
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.720
and other often used mainframe utilities.

86
00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.520
Again, the goal is really to make things easier,

87
00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.080
not to displace proven methods.

88
00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.720
Since ZOSMF is deeply integrated into the operating system,

89
00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:41.960
it's just there.

90
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.920
You'll have to take some steps to enable it,

91
00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.160
but you don't need to download or compile ZOSMF.

92
00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:50.720
And here's the thing, using a terminal and commands,

93
00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.440
there's a whole lot you can get into,

94
00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:58.560
but there's a lot that you may never need to get into,

95
00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.800
or you'll only need to do once ever.

96
00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:02.960
For that sort of thing,

97
00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.120
you're probably better off with a stack of books,

98
00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.800
a terminal, and a couple of hours

99
00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:08.760
blocked off on your calendar.

100
00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.640
But for everyday tasks with predictable variants

101
00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.760
that you might need to pull off once a month

102
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.200
or in preparation of a maintenance window,

103
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.600
that's the perfect opportunity to use ZOSMF workflows

104
00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:22.240
and automation to make sure you don't accidentally

105
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:23.920
forget a step or install something

106
00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:25.480
into the wrong directory.

107
00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:27.400
Not that any of us have ever done that.

108
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.480
All this to say, make sure ZOSMF is used wherever possible,

109
00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.080
along with any sort of automation and AI insight dashboards

110
00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.440
to make sure that the current state of your system

111
00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.480
matches your expectations of what's going on.

112
00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.600
When those get out of sync, that's where problems happen.

113
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.560
You'll be doing some ZOSMF work in the lab shortly,

114
00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.360
so I hope you're paying attention.
